window.SN_INDEX=[{"i": "excel-01", "t": "Getting Started with Excel for Data Analysis", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-01-getting-started-with-excel-for-data-analysis.html", "s": " Why Excel still matters in 2026 Even with Python, SQL, Power BI and AI assistants, Excel is still the first tool almost every analyst opens. It is where business teams keep budgets, targets, trackers and exports from other systems. If you can clean, analyse and present data in Excel quickly, you can add value from day one in any data role. Excel is also the easiest place to learn data thinking. Every idea you meet here comes back later: Idea in Excel Same idea later in the course A table with one header row A SQL table, a pandas DataFrame, a Power BI table SUMIFS, COUNTIFS GROUP BY in SQL, groupby() in pandas, DAX measures VLOOKUP / XLOOKUP SQL JOIN , pandas merge() , Power BI relationships Pivot table SQL aggregation, pivot_table() in pandas, Power BI matrix Conditional formatting Highlighting in dashboards Live class What was covered in class Live class 1 was the programme introduction. Live class 2 covered the basics of Excel, terminology, shortcuts, Excel properties, data types, data validation and formatting, and we started working on the first sheet. This lesson covers the first half; data validation and formatting continue in Lessons 2 and 3. The Excel window, part by part The main parts of the Excel window. The active cell is C4, and the formula bar shows the formula inside it. Part What it does Tip Ribbon Commands grouped into tabs: Home, Insert, Formulas, Data, Review, View Press Alt to see a key letter on every command Name Box Shows the address of the active cell"}, {"i": "excel-02", "t": "Data Entry, Data Validation & Data Cleaning", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-02-data-entry-data-validation-and-data-cleaning.html", "s": " Garbage in, garbage out Most time in real analytics is not spent on fancy charts; it is spent fixing data: extra spaces, inconsistent spellings ( Bangalore , Bengaluru , bengaluru ), numbers stored as text, duplicates and blanks. Surveys of data teams regularly report that cleaning and preparing data takes well over half of an analyst's time. There are two ways to win this fight: Prevent problems at entry time, and clean whatever slips through. Both are needed. Fast, consistent data entry AutoFill and the fill handle Select a cell and drag the small square at its bottom-right corner (the fill handle ). Excel continues the pattern it recognises. You type Drag gives you Jan Feb, Mar, Apr … Monday Tuesday, Wednesday … 1 , 2 (select both) 3, 4, 5 … Q1 Q2, Q3, Q4, Q1 … 01-04-2026 02-04-2026, 03-04-2026 … (right-drag for months or years) Item 1 Item 2, Item 3 … =B2*12 =B3*12 , =B4*12 … Double-click the fill handle to fill a formula down as far as the column next to it has data. This is the fastest way to copy a formula to 10,000 rows. Tip Custom lists File → Options → Advanced → Edit Custom Lists lets you add your own sequences, for example North, South, East, West or your company's department order. AutoFill and custom sort then follow that order. Flash Fill (Ctrl + E) Flash Fill learns a pattern from one or two examples you type. It is perfect for splitting names, extracting codes or reformatting text. X Flash Fill: type the first answer in each column, then press Ctrl + E A B C"}, {"i": "excel-03", "t": "Number, Custom & Conditional Formatting", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-03-number-custom-and-conditional-formatting.html", "s": " Formatting changes how a value looks, not what it is This is the most important idea in this lesson. When you format 0.256 as 26% , the cell still contains 0.256 . Every formula that uses the cell sees 0.256 . X Same stored value, different formats A B C 1 Stored value Format applied What you see 2 0.256 Percentage, 0 decimals 26% 3 0.256 Percentage, 1 decimal 25.6% 4 1,234,568 Number with separator 1,234,567.89 5 46139 Date 27-Apr-2026 6 46139 Long date Monday, 27 April 2026 7 0.6875 Time 16:30 C2 = TEXT (A2, &quot;0%&quot; ) C3 = TEXT (A3, &quot;0.0%&quot; ) C4 = TEXT (A4, &quot;#,##0.00&quot; ) C5 = TEXT (A5, &quot;dd-mmm-yyyy&quot; ) C6 = TEXT (A6, &quot;dddd, d mmmm yyyy&quot; ) C7 = TEXT (A7, &quot;hh:mm&quot; ) Column C uses the TEXT function to show exactly what each format produces. ( TEXT returns text, so use it for labels, not for numbers you still need to calculate with.) Watch out Rounding by format vs rounding by formula Formatting 2.456 with 1 decimal shows 2.5 but keeps 2.456 . Adding three such cells can show 7.4 while the visible numbers add up to 7.5 . If the rounded value must be used in calculations (invoices, tax), round it with a formula: =ROUND(A2,1) . Cell formatting the professional way Open Format Cells with Ctrl + 1 . It has six tabs: Number, Alignment, Font, Border, Fill, Protection. Goal How Bold header with fill colour Home → Bold ( Ctrl + B ) and Fill Color, or Cell Styles → Heading Wrap long text Home → Wrap Text ( Alt + H, W ) Centre a title"}, {"i": "excel-04", "t": "Formulas, Cell References & Aggregate Functions", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-04-formulas-cell-references-and-aggregate-functions.html", "s": " Formula vs function Definition Two words people mix up A formula is any calculation that starts with = . It can use cell references, numbers, operators and functions: =B2*C2 , =(B2-C2)/C2 , =SUM(B2:B10)*1.18 . A function is a named, ready-made calculation inside a formula: SUM , AVERAGE , IF . A function has a name, brackets and arguments separated by commas. Anatomy of a function call. Arguments in square brackets are optional. When you type a function name and ( , Excel shows a tooltip with its syntax, for example SUMIFS(sum_range, criteria_range1, criteria1, [criteria_range2, criteria2], …) . Press Ctrl + A after the bracket to open the Function Arguments dialog, which explains every argument and previews the result. Operators and order of calculation Operator Meaning Example Result + - * / Add, subtract, multiply, divide =10+4*2 18 ^ Power =2^10 1024 % Percent =500*18% 90 &amp; Join text =\"Q\"&amp;2 Q2 = &lt;&gt; &gt; &lt; &gt;= &lt;= Comparison, returns TRUE/FALSE =B2&gt;=100 TRUE or FALSE : Range A1:A10 10 cells , Union (inside functions) =SUM(A1:A3,C1:C3) Adds both ranges Excel follows the usual maths order, remembered as BODMAS/PEMDAS : brackets, then ^ , then * and / , then + and - , then &amp; , then comparisons. X Order of operations A B C 1 Formula Result Why 2 =10+4*2 18 Multiply first: 10 + 8 3 =(10+4)*2 28 Brackets first: 14 × 2 4 =2^3^2 64 Excel works left to right: (2^3)^2 = 64 5 =-2^2 4 Negation before power in Excel: (-2)^2 = 4 6 =100*(1+18%) 118 Add 18% GS"}, {"i": "excel-05", "t": "Text Functions, Logical Functions & IF", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-05-text-functions-logical-functions-and-if.html", "s": " Why text functions matter Real data arrives as text: order IDs like IN-2025-00042 , product codes, email addresses, \"Last, First\" names, addresses and free-text comments. Text functions let you pull out the part you need (the year, the region code, the domain) so you can analyse it. Measuring and extracting text Function What it returns Example Result LEN(text) Number of characters =LEN(\"Pune\") 4 LEFT(text, n) First n characters =LEFT(\"IN-2025-00042\",2) IN RIGHT(text, n) Last n characters =RIGHT(\"IN-2025-00042\",5) 00042 MID(text, start, n) n characters from position start =MID(\"IN-2025-00042\",4,4) 2025 FIND(find, text, [start]) Position of text (case-sensitive, no wildcards) =FIND(\"-\",\"IN-2025-00042\") 3 SEARCH(find, text, [start]) Position (not case-sensitive, wildcards allowed) =SEARCH(\"pune\",\"PUNE-411001\") 1 X Extracting parts of an order ID A B C D E F 1 Order ID Country =LEFT(A2,2) Year =MID(A2,4,4) Order no. =RIGHT(A2,5) Length Year as number 2 IN-2025-00042 IN 2025 00042 13 2025 3 IN-2023-01519 IN 2023 01519 13 2023 4 IN-2024-02988 IN 2024 02988 13 2024 B2 = LEFT (A2, 2 ) C2 = MID (A2, 4 , 4 ) D2 = RIGHT (A2, 5 ) E2 = LEN (A2) F2 = VALUE ( MID (A2, 4 , 4 )) Each pattern is copied down/across to the other cells in its column. Text functions always return text . MID(A2,4,4) returns the text \"2025\" , not the number 2025. Wrap with VALUE() (or multiply by 1) to get a number you can compare or add. When the position changes: FIND + MID Codes rarely have fixed lengths. Combi"}, {"i": "excel-06", "t": "Sorting, Filtering & Dynamic Arrays", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-06-sorting-filtering-and-dynamic-arrays.html", "s": " The sample table We will use this small sales table (sheet Reps , range A1:F9 ) so every result is easy to check by hand. Revenue is =D2*E2 . X Reps: sample sales table A B C D E F 1 Rep Region Product Units Price Revenue 2 Asha North Laptop 5 55,000 275,000 3 Ravi West Phone 12 18,000 216,000 4 Meera South Laptop 3 55,000 165,000 5 Kabir North Tablet 8 25,000 200,000 6 Zoya East Phone 15 18,000 270,000 7 Farhan West Laptop 4 55,000 220,000 8 Neha South Tablet 10 25,000 250,000 9 Rohan North Phone 9 18,000 162,000 F2 =D2*E2 Each pattern is copied down/across to the other cells in its column. Sorting Quick sort Click any cell in a column and use Data → Sort A to Z or Sort Z to A (or Home → Sort &amp; Filter). Excel detects the table around the cell and keeps each row together. Watch out Never sort a single selected column If you select only one column and sort, Excel asks whether to \"expand the selection\". Choosing \"Continue with the current selection\" sorts that column alone and scrambles your data: names no longer match their revenue. Always click a single cell inside the table, or select the whole table. Multi-level (level-wise) sort Data → Sort opens the Sort dialog, where you add levels. Excel sorts by the first level, then breaks ties with the next. The Sort dialog with two levels: Region A to Z, then Revenue largest to smallest. X Result: Region A→Z, then Revenue high→low A B C D 1 Rep Region Product Revenue 2 Zoya East Phone 270,000 3 Asha North Laptop 275,000 4 Kabir"}, {"i": "excel-07", "t": "Lookup Functions: VLOOKUP, XLOOKUP and INDEX-MATCH", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-07-lookup-functions-vlookup-xlookup-and-index-match.html", "s": " Why lookups matter Business data is split across tables: orders in one, products in another, customers in a third. A lookup fetches a value from one table using a key that both tables share, such as a product ID or employee ID. A lookup takes a key from one table, finds it in another table, and returns a value from the same row. Tip You are already learning SQL joins A lookup is a left join for one column at a time: keep every row of the main table and bring in matching information from the other table. You will see the same idea as JOIN in SQL (SQL Lesson 8), merge() in pandas, and relationships in Power BI. We will use this lookup table on a sheet called Prod : X Prod: product lookup table (A1:E7) A B C D E 1 Product ID Product Category Price Stock 2 P101 Office Chair Furniture 4500 32 3 P102 Standing Desk Furniture 18500 8 4 P103 Laser Printer Technology 12900 15 5 P104 Notebook Pack Office Supplies 240 540 6 P105 Smartphone Technology 21999 27 7 P106 Bookcase Furniture 7600 0 VLOOKUP =VLOOKUP(lookup_value, table_array, col_index_num, [range_lookup]) lookup_value : what you are looking for (the key). table_array : the table to search; the key must be in its first column . col_index_num : which column of that table to return (1 = first column). range_lookup : FALSE (or 0) for an exact match. Use TRUE only for approximate matching on sorted bands. X Order lines looking up product details with VLOOKUP A B C D E F 1 Order Product ID Qty Product =VLOOKUP(B2,Prod!$A$2:$E$7,2,FA"}, {"i": "excel-08", "t": "Pivot Tables from Basic to Advanced", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-08-pivot-tables-from-basic-to-advanced.html", "s": " What a pivot table does A pivot table groups rows by one or more fields and summarises a number for each group (sum, count, average…). It is the fastest way to answer \"how much, by what?\" questions, with no formulas. A pivot table turns thousands of order lines into a compact summary: rows grouped by Region, columns by Category, values summed. Creating a pivot table Step by step Step by step Make sure the data is structured (Lesson 1): one header row, no blank rows, no merged cells. Ideally convert it to a Table ( Ctrl + T ) so new rows are included automatically. Click any cell in the data → Insert → PivotTable (or Alt, N, V ). Choose New Worksheet → OK. An empty pivot appears with the PivotTable Fields pane on the right. Drag fields into the four areas: drag region to Rows , category to Columns , sales to Values . Right-click any number → Number Format → #,##0 so the format sticks when the pivot refreshes. The PivotTable Fields pane: a list of fields at the top, four areas at the bottom. Area What it does Example Rows One row per unique value Region, Category, Order Date Columns One column per unique value Year, Segment Values The numbers being summarised Sum of Sales, Count of Order ID Filters Filter the whole pivot by a field Segment = Corporate The pivot cache, Refresh and Refresh All A pivot table does not read your sheet directly. When you create it, Excel copies the data into a hidden pivot cache , and the pivot reads from that cache. This is why pivots are fast, and"}, {"i": "excel-09", "t": "Date, Time & Number Functions and Named Ranges", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-09-date-time-and-number-functions-and-named-ranges.html", "s": " Dates and times are numbers Excel stores a date as the number of days since 1 January 1900 (that day is 1). A time is a fraction of a day : 12:00 noon is 0.5 and 06:00 is 0.25. A date with a time is simply a whole number plus a fraction. X What is really stored A B C 1 You see Stored as Explanation 2 27-04-2026 46139 Days since 1-Jan-1900 3 12:00 0.5 Half a day 4 06:30 0.2708 6.5 hours ÷ 24 5 27-04-2026 18:00 46139.75 Date + time B2 =A2 B3 = TIME ( 12 , 0 , 0 ) B4 = TIME ( 6 , 30 , 0 ) B5 = DATE ( 2026 , 4 , 27 )+ TIME ( 18 , 0 , 0 ) This is why date arithmetic works: X Date arithmetic A B C D E 1 Order date Ship date Days to ship =B2-A2 Due date (+30 days) Overdue? (as of 15-Jun-2026) 2 27-04-2026 02-05-2026 5 27-05-2026 Overdue 3 20-05-2026 21-05-2026 1 19-06-2026 OK 4 01-06-2026 08-06-2026 7 01-07-2026 OK C2 =B2-A2 D2 =A2+ 30 E2 = IF ( DATE ( 2026 , 6 , 15 )&gt;D2, &quot;Overdue&quot; , &quot;OK&quot; ) Each pattern is copied down/across to the other cells in its column. Tip TODAY() and NOW() =TODAY() returns today's date and =NOW() the date and time. They are volatile : they recalculate every time the workbook changes, so results move every day. For reports that must not change, type the \"as of\" date into a cell and refer to it, as in the overdue example above. Shortcuts: Ctrl + ; types today's date as a fixed value. Building and taking apart dates Function Returns Example (A2 = 27-04-2026) Result DATE(y, m, d) A date from parts =DATE(2026,4,27) 27-04-2026 YEAR , MONTH ,"}, {"i": "excel-10", "t": "Charts and Advanced Charts", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-10-charts-and-advanced-charts.html", "s": " Start with the question, not the chart Choose a chart by the question you are answering. Creating and editing a chart Step by step The fast way Select the data including headers (or a summary table / pivot table). For a quick chart press Alt + F1 (embedded) or F11 (new chart sheet). Or use Insert → Recommended Charts to preview options. Click the chart: three buttons appear at its top-right: Chart Elements (+), Chart Styles (brush) and Chart Filters (funnel). Chart Design tab: Switch Row/Column, Select Data, Change Chart Type, Move Chart, Quick Layout. Format pane: double-click any element (axis, series, label) or press Ctrl + 1 with it selected. Element Good practice Title Say the insight: \"Technology drives 77% of sales\", not \"Sales by Category\" Axes Start bar/column axes at zero; format numbers (K, L, %); remove unnecessary decimals Gridlines Light grey or none Data labels Use them instead of an axis when there are few bars Legend Only if there is more than one series; better: label lines directly Colour One highlight colour for the point you want to make, grey for the rest Template Right-click → Save as Template to reuse your style The core chart types Here are the core charts built from Superstore data. The Excel steps follow each one. Column (comparison), line (trend), clustered and stacked bar (comparison of parts), and scatter (relationship). Chart Excel path Use when Clustered column / bar Insert → Column or Bar Comparing categories; use bar when labels are long or "}, {"i": "excel-11", "t": "Interactive Dashboard: Superstore Case Study", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-11-interactive-dashboard-superstore-case-study.html", "s": " The brief Case study Superstore sales review The Head of Sales wants a one-page Excel dashboard to review performance for 2023–2025. They want to see, at a glance: total sales, profit, profit margin and orders; how sales are trending; which regions, categories and sub-categories perform best and worst; and the effect of discounts on profit. They want to filter by year, region and segment. The dashboard will be emailed every month, so it must be protected against accidental changes. Step 0: questions and KPIs first KPI / question Calculation Visual Total sales Sum of sales KPI card Total profit and margin Sum of profit; profit ÷ sales KPI cards Number of orders Distinct count of order_id KPI card Sales trend Sales by month Line chart Regional performance Sales and profit by region Bar chart Product mix Sales by category and sub-category Bar chart / treemap Discount impact Profit by discount level Column chart Filters Year, Region, Segment Timeline + slicers Agreeing on this table before building saves hours of rework. It is also your \"requirements document\" in interviews. Here is the finished result we are aiming for (drawn from the real data): The finished Superstore dashboard: KPI cards on top, trend and breakdown charts below, slicers on the left. Step 1: prepare the data Import superstore_sales.csv with Data → From Text/CSV (Power Query, Lesson 12), or open it and convert the range to a Table named tblSales ( Ctrl + T ). Add helper columns that the pivots will need: Colum"}, {"i": "excel-12", "t": "Power Query, Power Pivot, Analysis ToolPak & What-If Analysis", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-12-power-query-power-pivot-analysis-toolpak-and-what-if-analysi.html", "s": " Why \"Power\" tools? Formulas and pivots are great until the same cleaning has to be repeated every month, the data has several related tables, or it grows past a million rows. The Power tools solve exactly these problems, and they are the same engines used in Power BI , so everything you learn here transfers directly. The modern Excel analytics stack. Power Query and the Data Model are shared with Power BI. Power Query: repeatable cleaning Power Query (Data → Get &amp; Transform Data ) imports data and records every cleaning step you click. Next month, put the new file in place and click Refresh : all steps run again automatically. Step by step Your first query Data → Get Data → From File → From Text/CSV → choose superstore_sales.csv → Transform Data . The Power Query Editor opens. Check data types in each column header (ABC = text, 123 = whole number, 1.2 = decimal, calendar = date). Fix any wrong ones (right-click header → Change Type). Remove columns you do not need (select → right-click → Remove, or Choose Columns ). Filter rows with the header drop-downs (for example remove test orders). Add a column: Add Column → Date → Year , or Custom Column with a formula such as [sales] - [profit] . Rename the query (right panel) to Sales → Home → Close &amp; Load To… → Table, PivotTable report, or Only Create Connection + Add to Data Model . The Power Query Editor. Every action you take is recorded in Applied Steps on the right. Transformations you will use most Transformation Wher"}, {"i": "sql-01", "t": "Databases, RDBMS & SQL Basics", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-01-databases-rdbms-and-sql-basics.html", "s": " What is a database? A database is an organised collection of data stored so that it can be found, updated and analysed efficiently and safely by many people at the same time. A DBMS (database management system) is the software that manages it. Your bank balance, every Swiggy order, every Aadhaar-linked record and every hospital appointment lives in a database. Spreadsheets are personal tools; databases are shared systems built for scale, safety and many users. Types of databases Type Organises data as Examples Typical use Relational (RDBMS) Tables with rows and columns, linked by keys SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle, SQLite Orders, payments, HR, finance: most business data Document JSON-like documents MongoDB, Cosmos DB Product catalogues, app content Key-value Key → value pairs Redis, DynamoDB Caching, sessions Column-family / warehouse Columns, built for analytics Snowflake, BigQuery, Redshift, Databricks SQL Analytics on huge data Graph Nodes and edges Neo4j Social networks, fraud rings Vector Number vectors (embeddings) pgvector, Pinecone AI search and RAG (GenAI track) This track focuses on relational databases and SQL , the language almost every data role uses daily. You will practise in Microsoft SQL Server with SSMS , as in class. The SQL you learn transfers to PostgreSQL, MySQL, Snowflake and BigQuery with small differences in functions. How a relational database organises data The hierarchy inside SQL Server: a server holds databases, a database holds schemas"}, {"i": "sql-02", "t": "ER Diagrams, Relationships, Keys & Normalisation", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-02-er-diagrams-relationships-keys-and-normalisation.html", "s": " Why design matters A database that is designed badly is slow, wastes space and, worst of all, lets data contradict itself. Imagine the customer's city stored in every order row: if the customer moves, you must update thousands of rows, and missing one creates two different \"truths\". Good design stores each fact once and connects tables with keys . Entity–Relationship (ER) diagrams An ER diagram is a picture of the data model before any table is created. Definition Building blocks Entity : a real-world thing you store data about: Customer, Order, Product, Employee. Becomes a table . Attribute : a property of an entity: name, city, price. Becomes a column . Relationship : how entities are associated: a Customer places Orders. Implemented with foreign keys . Cardinality : how many of one entity relate to another: one-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-many. Participation (optionality) : whether the relationship is mandatory (every order must have a customer) or optional (a customer may have zero orders). Crow's-foot notation The most common notation in industry draws symbols at the ends of each relationship line: Crow's-foot symbols. Read each end as \"minimum … maximum\". Reading example: Customer ||——o&lt; Order means \"each order belongs to exactly one customer; each customer places zero or many orders\". Types of relationships One-to-one (1:1) Each row in A matches at most one row in B. Example: Employee ↔ Employee_Bank_Details. Often used to split sensitive or rarely used columns in"}, {"i": "sql-03", "t": "Data Types, Operators, NULLs, Variables & Comments", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-03-data-types-operators-nulls-variables-and-comments.html", "s": " Why data types matter Every column has exactly one data type . The type decides what values are allowed, how much storage they use, how they sort and compare, and which functions work on them. A column of dates stored as text sorts \"01-02-2026\" before \"31-01-2025\" and cannot be used for date arithmetic, the same problem you met in Excel. SQL Server data types The main families of SQL Server data types. Numbers Type Range / precision Storage Use for TINYINT 0 to 255 1 byte Ratings, small codes SMALLINT −32,768 to 32,767 2 bytes Small counts INT about ±2.1 billion 4 bytes IDs, quantities (default choice) BIGINT about ±9.2 × 10¹⁸ 8 bytes Very large IDs, event logs DECIMAL(p, s) / NUMERIC exact, p digits with s after the point 5–17 bytes Money , rates: DECIMAL(12,2) holds up to 9,999,999,999.99 MONEY exact, 4 decimals 8 bytes Currency (DECIMAL is more portable) FLOAT , REAL approximate binary floating point 8 / 4 bytes Scientific measurements, ML scores BIT 0, 1 or NULL 1 bit Yes/No flags Watch out Never store money in FLOAT FLOAT cannot represent many decimal fractions exactly (0.1 + 0.2 is not exactly 0.3), so totals drift by tiny amounts. Use DECIMAL(p, s) for currency. Text Type Description Example CHAR(n) Fixed length, padded with spaces CHAR(2) for state codes MH VARCHAR(n) Variable length, up to n bytes (n ≤ 8000) VARCHAR(100) for names in English NVARCHAR(n) Variable length Unicode (Hindi, Tamil, emoji) NVARCHAR(100) for names in any language VARCHAR(MAX) , NVARCHAR(MAX)"}, {"i": "sql-04", "t": "DDL, DML & Constraints: CREATE, ALTER, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-04-ddl-dml-and-constraints-create-alter-insert-update-delete.html", "s": " The life cycle of a table DDL creates and changes structure; DML fills and changes the rows inside it. CREATE TABLE SQL Copy CREATE TABLE departments ( dept_id INT PRIMARY KEY , dept_name VARCHAR ( 50 ) NOT NULL UNIQUE , location VARCHAR ( 50 ) DEFAULT &#x27;Bengaluru&#x27; ); CREATE TABLE staff ( emp_id INT PRIMARY KEY , full_name VARCHAR ( 100 ) NOT NULL , email VARCHAR ( 100 ) NOT NULL UNIQUE , dept_id INT NOT NULL REFERENCES departments (dept_id), salary DECIMAL ( 10 , 2 ) CHECK (salary &gt; 0 ), join_date DATE DEFAULT CURRENT_DATE, is_active BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE ); Result Commands completed successfully. Commands completed successfully. In SQL Server you would usually write IDENTITY(1,1) for an auto-numbered key, BIT for the flag and DEFAULT GETDATE() for today. Here is the same staff table in full T-SQL style, with named constraints (names make error messages and later changes much easier): SQL Copy CREATE TABLE dbo.staff ( emp_id INT IDENTITY ( 1 , 1 ) NOT NULL , full_name NVARCHAR ( 100 ) NOT NULL , email VARCHAR ( 100 ) NOT NULL , dept_id INT NOT NULL , salary DECIMAL ( 10 , 2 ) NULL , join_date DATE NOT NULL CONSTRAINT df_staff_join DEFAULT ( CAST ( GETDATE () AS DATE )), is_active BIT NOT NULL CONSTRAINT df_staff_active DEFAULT ( 1 ), CONSTRAINT pk_staff PRIMARY KEY (emp_id), CONSTRAINT uq_staff_email UNIQUE (email), CONSTRAINT fk_staff_dept FOREIGN KEY (dept_id) REFERENCES dbo.departments (dept_id), CONSTRAINT ck_staff_salary CHECK (salary &gt; 0 ) ); Constraints"}, {"i": "sql-05", "t": "SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY & Filtering Operators", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-05-select-where-order-by-and-filtering-operators.html", "s": " The shape of a SELECT statement SQL Copy SELECT column_list -- what to show FROM table_name -- where it comes from WHERE row_conditions -- which rows to keep GROUP BY grouping_columns -- (Lesson 7) HAVING group_conditions -- (Lesson 7) ORDER BY sort_columns; -- how to sort the result Only SELECT is required ( SELECT 1 + 1; works). Everything else is optional but must appear in this order. SELECT: columns, expressions and aliases SQL Copy SELECT TOP 5 order_id, order_date, customer_name, sales, profit, sales - profit AS cost, ROUND ( 100.0 * profit / sales, 1 ) AS margin_pct, &#x27;INR&#x27; AS currency FROM superstore; Result order_id order_date customer_name sales profit cost margin_pct currency IN-2023-00001 2023-01-01 Ishaan Gupta 36,000 3,472.55 32,527.45 9.6 INR IN-2023-00001 2023-01-01 Ishaan Gupta 3,850 1,169.54 2,680.46 30.4 INR IN-2023-00001 2023-01-01 Ishaan Gupta 62,080 6,044.47 56,035.53 9.7 INR IN-2023-00002 2023-01-03 Kabir Singh 5,216 450.16 4,765.84 8.6 INR IN-2023-00003 2023-01-03 Sneha Khan 24,591 -5,512.33 30,103.33 -22.4 INR 5 rows × 8 columns SELECT * returns every column. Use it to explore, never in production code (it breaks when columns change and reads more data than needed). An alias renames a column or table in the result: AS margin_pct . AS is optional ( sales - profit cost ) but makes code clearer. Aliases with spaces need brackets or quotes: AS [Margin %] . Expressions can use operators, functions and literals. Table aliases SQL Copy SELECT s.or"}, {"i": "sql-06", "t": "SQL Functions: Aggregate, Text, Date & Number", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-06-sql-functions-aggregate-text-date-and-number.html", "s": " Two kinds of functions Scalar functions return one value per row. Aggregate functions collapse many rows into one value (per group). Aggregate functions Function Returns NULL handling COUNT(*) Number of rows Counts every row COUNT(col) Number of non-NULL values Skips NULLs COUNT(DISTINCT col) Number of distinct non-NULL values Skips NULLs SUM(col) Total Skips NULLs AVG(col) Average of non-NULL values Skips NULLs (they are not treated as 0) MIN(col) , MAX(col) Smallest / largest (numbers, text, dates) Skips NULLs STDEV(col) , VAR(col) Sample standard deviation / variance Skips NULLs STRING_AGG(col, sep) Values joined into one string Skips NULLs SQL Copy SELECT COUNT (*) AS order_lines, COUNT ( DISTINCT order_id) AS orders, COUNT ( DISTINCT customer_id) AS customers, ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) AS total_sales, ROUND ( AVG (sales), 0 ) AS avg_line_sales, MIN (order_date) AS first_order, MAX (order_date) AS last_order, ROUND ( STDEV (sales), 0 ) AS sd_sales FROM superstore; Result order_lines orders customers total_sales avg_line_sales first_order last_order sd_sales 6323 3200 785 201,413,569 31,854 2023-01-01 2025-12-31 60,414 1 rows × 8 columns SQL Copy SELECT category, STRING_AGG (sub_category, &#x27;, &#x27; ) AS sub_categories FROM ( SELECT DISTINCT category, sub_category FROM superstore) AS t GROUP BY category ORDER BY category; Result category sub_categories Furniture Bookcases, Chairs, Tables, Furnishings Office Supplies Appliances, Art, Binders, Storage, Paper Technology P"}, {"i": "sql-07", "t": "GROUP BY, HAVING, CASE WHEN, ROLLUP, CUBE & COALESCE", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-07-group-by-having-case-when-rollup-cube-and-coalesce.html", "s": " GROUP BY: one row per group GROUP BY collapses rows that share the same value(s) into one row, and aggregate functions summarise each group. It is the SQL version of a pivot table (Excel Lesson 8) and of groupby() in pandas. GROUP BY region: rows with the same region are collected into one group, then SUM is computed for each group. SQL Copy SELECT region, COUNT ( DISTINCT order_id) AS orders, ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) AS sales, ROUND ( SUM (profit), 0 ) AS profit, ROUND ( 100.0 * SUM (profit) / SUM (sales), 1 ) AS margin_pct FROM superstore GROUP BY region ORDER BY sales DESC ; Result region orders sales profit margin_pct South 831 53,952,769 4,702,012 8.7 West 811 51,958,521 4,719,833 9.1 North 792 49,018,161 4,555,830 9.3 East 766 46,484,119 4,374,490 9.4 4 rows × 5 columns Grouping by several columns SQL Copy SELECT category, sub_category, COUNT (*) AS lines, ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) AS sales, ROUND ( SUM (profit), 0 ) AS profit FROM superstore GROUP BY category, sub_category ORDER BY category, sales DESC ; Result category sub_category lines sales profit Furniture Tables 505 14,046,224 -816,042 Furniture Bookcases 477 11,274,849 -422,786 Furniture Chairs 493 8,091,474 342,043 Furniture Furnishings 442 1,429,307 78,632 Office Supplies Appliances 492 6,333,529 944,927 Office Supplies Storage 507 2,659,340 397,228 Office Supplies Paper 559 493,232 75,040 Office Supplies Binders 494 401,219 61,074 Office Supplies Art 462 245,985 36,526 Technology Copiers 468 78,749,926 8,436,"}, {"i": "sql-08", "t": "Joins and a Retail Case Study", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-08-joins-and-a-retail-case-study.html", "s": " Why joins? In a normalised database (Lesson 2) customer details live in customers , products in products , and orders in orders and order_items . To answer \"sales by customer segment and product category\" you must join them back together on their keys. A join is SQL's version of XLOOKUP (Excel Lesson 7) and pandas merge() . A tiny example first Two small tables make every join type easy to see: SQL Copy CREATE TABLE students (student_id INT PRIMARY KEY , name VARCHAR ( 20 )); INSERT INTO students VALUES ( 1 , &#x27;Asha&#x27; ), ( 2 , &#x27;Ravi&#x27; ), ( 3 , &#x27;Meera&#x27; ), ( 4 , &#x27;Kabir&#x27; ); CREATE TABLE enrolments (student_id INT , course VARCHAR ( 20 )); INSERT INTO enrolments VALUES ( 1 , &#x27;SQL&#x27; ), ( 1 , &#x27;Python&#x27; ), ( 2 , &#x27;Excel&#x27; ), ( 5 , &#x27;Power BI&#x27; ); SELECT &#x27;students&#x27; AS t, COUNT (*) AS n FROM students UNION ALL SELECT &#x27;enrolments&#x27; , COUNT (*) FROM enrolments; Result Commands completed successfully. (4 rows affected) Commands completed successfully. (4 rows affected) t n students 4 enrolments 4 2 rows × 2 columns Asha (1) has two enrolments, Ravi (2) has one, Meera (3) and Kabir (4) have none, and enrolment for student 5 has no matching student (an \"orphan\" row). The join types as Venn diagrams. Shaded areas are the rows returned (A = left table, B = right table). INNER JOIN: only matches SQL Copy SELECT s.student_id, s.name, e.course FROM students AS s INNER JOIN enrolments AS e ON e.student_id "}, {"i": "sql-09", "t": "Set Operators: UNION, UNION ALL, INTERSECT & EXCEPT", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-09-set-operators-union-union-all-intersect-and-except.html", "s": " Joins add columns; set operators add rows A join puts tables side by side (more columns). A set operator stacks or compares the rows of two query results with the same columns. The four set operators. A and B are the result sets of two queries. Rules for set operators Both queries must return the same number of columns . Corresponding columns must have compatible data types (text with text, numbers with numbers). Column names come from the first query . Only one ORDER BY , at the very end, applies to the combined result. UNION , INTERSECT and EXCEPT remove duplicates; UNION ALL does not. UNION ALL: append UNION ALL stacks results exactly as they are, like Power Query's Append or pasting one table under another. It is the fastest set operator because it does not check for duplicates. SQL Copy SELECT &#x27;Furniture&#x27; AS source, COUNT (*) AS lines, ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) AS sales FROM superstore WHERE category = &#x27;Furniture&#x27; UNION ALL SELECT &#x27;Technology&#x27; , COUNT (*), ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) FROM superstore WHERE category = &#x27;Technology&#x27; UNION ALL SELECT &#x27;Office Supplies&#x27; , COUNT (*), ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) FROM superstore WHERE category = &#x27;Office Supplies&#x27; ORDER BY sales DESC ; Result source lines sales Technology 1892 156,438,413 Furniture 1917 34,841,853 Office Supplies 2514 10,133,304 3 rows × 3 columns A very common real use: combining monthly or regional tables with the same structure. SQL Copy SELECT * FROM sales_j"}, {"i": "sql-10", "t": "Subqueries, Derived Tables & EXISTS", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-10-subqueries-derived-tables-and-exists.html", "s": " What is a subquery? A subquery (inner query, nested query) is a SELECT written inside another statement, in brackets. The outer query uses its result. Where subqueries can appear and what they return. By what it returns Example Used with Scalar (one value) (SELECT AVG(sales) FROM superstore) =, &gt;, &lt; … or as a column Multi-row (one column, many rows) (SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE …) IN , NOT IN , ANY , ALL , EXISTS Table (many columns and rows) (SELECT region, SUM(sales) … GROUP BY region) FROM as a derived table Scalar subqueries \"Which order lines are above the average line value?\" SQL Copy SELECT COUNT (*) AS lines_above_average, ( SELECT ROUND ( AVG (sales), 0 ) FROM superstore) AS average_line_sales FROM superstore WHERE sales &gt; ( SELECT AVG (sales) FROM superstore); Result lines_above_average average_line_sales 1603 31,854 1 rows × 2 columns The inner query runs first and returns one number; the outer query compares each row with it. You cannot write WHERE sales &gt; AVG(sales) directly, because aggregates are not allowed in WHERE (Lesson 5's processing order). \"Show each region's sales and its share of the grand total\": SQL Copy SELECT region, ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) AS sales, ROUND ( 100.0 * SUM (sales) / ( SELECT SUM (sales) FROM superstore), 1 ) AS pct_of_total FROM superstore GROUP BY region ORDER BY sales DESC ; Result region sales pct_of_total South 53,952,769 26.8 West 51,958,521 25.8 North 49,018,161 24.3 East 46,484,119 23.1 4 rows × 3 column"}, {"i": "sql-11", "t": "OFFSET-FETCH and Window Functions I: OVER, PARTITION BY & Ranking", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-11-offset-fetch-and-window-functions-i-over-partition-by-and-ra.html", "s": " OFFSET and FETCH: pagination OFFSET n ROWS FETCH NEXT m ROWS ONLY skips n rows and returns the next m. It requires ORDER BY . It is the standard (ANSI) way to page results, used for \"page 3 of results\" in applications and for \"rows 11 to 20\" questions. SQL Copy SELECT customer_name, city, segment FROM customers ORDER BY customer_id OFFSET 10 ROWS FETCH NEXT 5 ROWS ONLY ; Result customer_name city segment Sai Iyer Indore Consumer Neha Kulkarni Bengaluru Consumer Diya Khan Bhubaneswar Home Office Kabir Das Kolkata Consumer Reyansh Joshi Mumbai Corporate 5 rows × 3 columns Page OFFSET FETCH 1 0 page size 2 page size page size page p (p − 1) × page size page size TOP n cannot skip rows; OFFSET-FETCH can. PostgreSQL/MySQL write LIMIT m OFFSET n . On very deep pages (OFFSET 1,000,000) databases still read and discard all skipped rows, so large systems use keyset pagination ( WHERE id &gt; last_seen_id ORDER BY id ). The \"Nth highest value\" question can be answered with it too: the second-highest line value is ORDER BY sales DESC OFFSET 1 ROWS FETCH NEXT 1 ROWS ONLY on distinct values: SQL Copy SELECT DISTINCT sales AS second_highest_sales FROM superstore ORDER BY sales DESC OFFSET 1 ROWS FETCH NEXT 1 ROWS ONLY ; Result second_highest_sales 551,530 1 rows × 1 columns What is a window function? A window function computes a value for each row using a set of related rows (its window ), without collapsing the rows like GROUP BY does. GROUP BY collapses rows into one per group. A window"}, {"i": "sql-12", "t": "Window Functions II: Running Totals, LEAD, LAG & Frames", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-12-window-functions-ii-running-totals-lead-lag-and-frames.html", "s": " Window frames Inside OVER , ORDER BY defines the order and an optional frame defines which rows around the current row the function uses. SQL Copy SUM (x) OVER ( PARTITION BY p ORDER BY d ROWS BETWEEN &lt;start&gt; AND &lt; end &gt;) Frame boundary Meaning UNBOUNDED PRECEDING From the first row of the partition n PRECEDING n rows before the current row CURRENT ROW The current row n FOLLOWING n rows after UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING To the last row of the partition Frames relative to the current row (highlighted). A running total uses everything up to the current row; a 3-row moving average uses the previous two rows plus the current one. Common mistakes The default frame trap When ORDER BY is present and no frame is written, SQL Server uses RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW . RANGE treats rows with the same ORDER BY value as peers and includes them all, which gives surprising results with ties, and LAST_VALUE then returns the current row's value. For running totals always write ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW explicitly; it is also much faster. Running totals First build the monthly sales table, then add a running (cumulative) total and a share: SQL Copy WITH monthly AS ( SELECT YEAR (order_date) AS yr, MONTH (order_date) AS mth, SUM (sales) AS sales FROM superstore GROUP BY YEAR (order_date), MONTH (order_date) ) SELECT yr, mth, ROUND (sales, 0 ) AS sales, ROUND ( SUM (sales) OVER ( PARTITION BY yr ORDER BY mth ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND C"}, {"i": "sql-13", "t": "CTEs, Views, Temporary Tables, Stored Procedures & Functions", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-13-ctes-views-temporary-tables-stored-procedures-and-functions.html", "s": " CTE: a named, readable subquery A Common Table Expression is a named result set defined with WITH that exists for one statement . It makes complex logic read top to bottom, like paragraphs, instead of inside-out. SQL Copy WITH name1 AS ( SELECT … ), name2 AS ( SELECT … FROM name1 … -- later CTEs can use earlier ones ) SELECT … FROM name2 …; From nested subqueries to CTEs Question: \"Which regions had above-average 2025 sales per customer?\" SQL Copy WITH customer_sales AS ( -- step 1: sales per customer in 2025 SELECT c.region, c.customer_id, SUM (oi.sales) AS sales FROM order_items AS oi JOIN orders AS o ON o.order_id = oi.order_id JOIN customers AS c ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id WHERE o.order_date &gt;= &#x27;2025-01-01&#x27; AND o.order_date &lt; &#x27;2026-01-01&#x27; GROUP BY c.region, c.customer_id ), region_avg AS ( -- step 2: average per region SELECT region, COUNT (*) AS customers, AVG (sales) AS avg_sales_per_customer FROM customer_sales GROUP BY region ) SELECT region, customers, ROUND (avg_sales_per_customer, 0 ) AS avg_sales_per_customer, ROUND (( SELECT AVG (sales) FROM customer_sales), 0 ) AS overall_avg FROM region_avg WHERE avg_sales_per_customer &gt; ( SELECT AVG (sales) FROM customer_sales) -- step 3: compare ORDER BY avg_sales_per_customer DESC ; Result region customers avg_sales_per_customer overall_avg West 156 142,513 125,805 South 160 130,010 125,805 2 rows × 4 columns customer_sales is used twice , which with plain subqueries would mean copy-pastin"}, {"i": "sql-14", "t": "Transactions, Indexes, Performance & Security", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-14-transactions-indexes-performance-and-security.html", "s": " Transactions and ACID A transaction is a group of statements treated as one unit of work: all succeed (commit) or none do (rollback) . Moving ₹5,000 between two accounts is two updates; if the server crashes between them, money would vanish without a transaction. A bank transfer as a transaction. Either both updates are saved, or both are undone. Property Meaning Example A tomicity All or nothing Both account updates happen, or neither C onsistency Data stays valid (constraints hold) Total money before = total after; no negative balance if a CHECK forbids it I solation Concurrent transactions do not see each other's half-finished work Another user does not see the debit without the credit D urability Once committed, it survives a crash Transaction log is written to disk first Transaction commands Command Purpose BEGIN TRANSACTION ( BEGIN TRAN ) Start a transaction COMMIT Make all changes permanent ROLLBACK Undo everything since BEGIN (or since a savepoint) SAVE TRANSACTION name Create a savepoint inside the transaction ROLLBACK TRANSACTION name Undo back to the savepoint only @@TRANCOUNT How many transactions are open (should be 0 when you leave) By default SQL Server runs in autocommit mode: every single statement is its own transaction. Wrap several statements in BEGIN TRAN … COMMIT to group them. Seeing a rollback SQL Copy CREATE TABLE accounts (acct_id INT PRIMARY KEY , holder VARCHAR ( 20 ), balance DECIMAL ( 12 , 2 )); INSERT INTO accounts VALUES ( 1 , &#x27;Asha&#x27;"}, {"i": "sql-15", "t": "End-to-End SQL Case Study & Interview Practice", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-15-end-to-end-sql-case-study-and-interview-practice.html", "s": " The business problem Case study Retail growth review You are the data analyst at an Indian retailer selling Furniture, Office Supplies and Technology through four regions. Sales grew from 2023 to 2025, but the CFO suspects profit is not keeping pace. Management wants answers to five questions: (1) Is the data trustworthy? (2) How are sales and profit really trending? (3) Where are we losing money and why? (4) Who are our best customers and are they staying? (5) What should we do next quarter? A repeatable SQL analysis workflow. Step 1: understand the data SQL Copy SELECT &#x27;orders&#x27; AS tbl, COUNT (*) AS n, MIN (order_date) AS first_date, MAX (order_date) AS last_date FROM orders UNION ALL SELECT &#x27;order_items&#x27; , COUNT (*), NULL , NULL FROM order_items UNION ALL SELECT &#x27;customers&#x27; , COUNT (*), NULL , NULL FROM customers UNION ALL SELECT &#x27;products&#x27; , COUNT (*), NULL , NULL FROM products; Result tbl n first_date last_date orders 3200 2023-01-01 2025-12-31 order_items 6323 NULL NULL customers 800 NULL NULL products 78 NULL NULL 4 rows × 4 columns Grain : order_items has one row per product line in an order; orders one row per order; customers one row per customer. KPIs like \"orders\" must use COUNT(DISTINCT order_id) , and \"average order value\" needs order-level totals (Lesson 10). Step 2: data-quality checks Always check before analysing. A compact data-quality report: SQL Copy SELECT ( SELECT COUNT (*) FROM order_items WHERE sales IS NULL OR "}, {"i": "powerbi-01", "t": "Getting Started with Power BI & Your First HR Report", "k": "Power BI", "f": "lessons/powerbi-01-getting-started-with-power-bi-and-your-first-hr-report.html", "s": " What is Power BI? Power BI is Microsoft's business-intelligence platform: it connects to data, cleans and models it, and turns it into interactive reports and dashboards that people can share. In 2026 it is part of Microsoft Fabric , Microsoft's all-in-one analytics platform, and it is one of the most requested tools in analyst job posts, alongside Excel, SQL and Tableau. The Power BI components. You build in Desktop, publish to the Service, and consume on the web or mobile. Component What it is Who uses it Power BI Desktop Free Windows application to build data models and reports ( .pbix ) Analysts, developers Power BI Service (app.powerbi.com) Cloud site to publish, share, schedule refresh, create dashboards and apps Analysts and report consumers Power BI Mobile iOS/Android apps to view reports and get alerts Managers on the go Power BI Report Builder Paginated, print-ready reports (invoices, statements) Report developers On-premises data gateway Secure bridge so the cloud can refresh data from your own servers Admins Power BI Report Server Self-hosted option for organisations that cannot use the cloud IT Copilot AI assistant that writes DAX, builds report pages and summarises insights (needs a Fabric/Premium capacity) Everyone Tip Report vs dashboard In Power BI a report is one or more pages of interactive visuals built on a data model. A dashboard (Service only) is a single page of pinned tiles from different reports. Many teams use reports only, or share them as an app "}, {"i": "powerbi-02", "t": "Power Query, Star Schema & Data Modelling (Adventure Cycles)", "k": "Power BI", "f": "lessons/powerbi-02-power-query-star-schema-and-data-modelling-adventure-cycles.html", "s": " Why modelling matters more than visuals Beautiful visuals on a bad model give wrong or slow answers. In Power BI the data model decides what numbers mean: how tables relate, which table filters which, and how fast DAX runs. The golden rule: model as a star schema . A star schema: one central fact table of events/numbers surrounded by descriptive dimension tables. Filters flow from dimensions (one side) to the fact (many side). Fact table Dimension table Describes Events or transactions (a sale, a click, a shipment) The \"who, what, where, when\" of those events Rows Many (millions) Fewer (thousands) Columns Keys + numeric measures (quantity, amount, cost) Keys + descriptive attributes (name, category, city) Changes Grows constantly Changes rarely Example fact_sales dim_product , dim_customer , dim_date Why a star beats one big flat table: smaller file (text stored once), faster visuals, one place to fix a name, correct filtering across several facts, and simpler DAX. A snowflake (dimensions normalised into sub-dimensions) works but adds joins; prefer a star in Power BI. The Adventure Cycles dataset A fictional bicycle company sells bikes, components, clothing and accessories in India, the UAE and Singapore. The model has one fact table and four dimensions (plus the date table): Python Copy import pandas as pd D = &quot;adventure_cycles/&quot; fact = pd.read_csv (D + &quot;fact_sales.csv&quot; , parse_dates=[ &quot;order_date&quot; ]); prod = pd.read_csv (D + &quot;dim_product."}, {"i": "powerbi-03", "t": "DAX Fundamentals: Measures, Filter Context & CALCULATE", "k": "Power BI", "f": "lessons/powerbi-03-dax-fundamentals-measures-filter-context-and-calculate.html", "s": " What is DAX? DAX (Data Analysis Expressions) is the formula language used in Power BI, Excel's Data Model and Analysis Services. It looks like Excel formulas, but it works on columns and tables and recalculates for whatever the user selects in a visual. DAX is the skill that separates a report builder from a Power BI developer. Calculated column Measure Evaluated Row by row when data is refreshed On the fly , for the current filter selection Stored in the model Yes (uses memory) No (only the formula) Result depends on slicers? No Yes Use for New attributes to slice by (Age Band, Hire Year, Profit per row) Totals, ratios, KPIs, growth Appears in Rows, columns, slicers, filters Values area, cards, tooltips The same table, two ways. A calculated column adds a value to every row. A measure returns one number computed from whichever rows are currently filtered. Tip Rule of thumb If you want to put it on an axis or slicer , make a column. If you want to put it in the Values well , make a measure. In a star schema, almost every number is a measure. DAX syntax in five minutes DAX Copy Total Sales = SUM ( fact_sales[sales_amount] ) Total Sales is the measure name (spaces allowed, no brackets when defining it). = starts the formula. SUM is a function ; fact_sales[sales_amount] is a fully qualified column (table name, then column in square brackets). Always include the table name for columns; never include it for measures: [Total Sales] . Strings use double quotes; comments use -- or /"}, {"i": "powerbi-04", "t": "DAX Time Intelligence, Ranking, Parameters & Dynamic Measures", "k": "Power BI", "f": "lessons/powerbi-04-dax-time-intelligence-ranking-parameters-and-dynamic-measure.html", "s": " What time intelligence needs Time-intelligence functions move or expand the date filter : \"this year to date\", \"the same period last year\". They only work when: You have a date table with every date (Lesson 2), it is marked as a date table , and it is related to the fact table's date column. Always use the date table's Date column inside time functions, never the fact table's date. Time intelligence changes the date filter, then CALCULATE re-evaluates the measure. SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR shifts the selected dates back exactly one year. Core measures DAX Copy Total Sales = SUM ( fact_sales[sales_amount] ) -- Cumulative totals Sales YTD = TOTALYTD ( [Total Sales], dim_date[date] ) Sales QTD = TOTALQTD ( [Total Sales], dim_date[date] ) Sales MTD = TOTALMTD ( [Total Sales], dim_date[date] ) -- Indian financial year ending 31 March: Sales FYTD = TOTALYTD ( [Total Sales], dim_date[date], &quot;31-3&quot; ) -- Previous periods Sales LY = CALCULATE ( [Total Sales], SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR ( dim_date[date] ) ) Sales PM = CALCULATE ( [Total Sales], DATEADD ( dim_date[date], - 1 , MONTH ) ) Sales PQ = CALCULATE ( [Total Sales], DATEADD ( dim_date[date], - 1 , QUARTER ) ) -- Growth YoY % = DIVIDE ( [Total Sales] - [Sales LY], [Sales LY] ) MoM % = DIVIDE ( [Total Sales] - [Sales PM], [Sales PM] ) YoY Δ = [Total Sales] - [Sales LY] Function Returns Notes TOTALYTD ( expr, dates, [year_end] ) Year-to-date total Shortcut for CALCULATE ( expr, DATESYTD ( dates ) ) DATESYTD / DATESQTD / DATESMTD The dat"}, {"i": "powerbi-05", "t": "Visuals, Filters, Drill-through, Tooltips, Buttons & Report Design", "k": "Power BI", "f": "lessons/powerbi-05-visuals-filters-drill-through-tooltips-buttons-and-report-de.html", "s": " Choosing the visual Match the visual to the question. Visual Fields Good for Watch out Card / multi-row card Values One or few headline numbers Format display units (K, M, Cr) and add a clear label Clustered bar / column Axis, Values, Legend Comparisons Sort descending, start at zero, ≤ 15 bars Stacked / 100% stacked Axis, Legend, Values Composition Only the bottom segment is easy to compare Line / area X-axis (date), Y-axis, Legend Trends Limit to ~5 lines; use a continuous date axis Line + clustered column Shared axis, column values, line values Two measures with different scales (secondary axis) Label both axes Waterfall Category, Y-axis Bridge from start to end (profit walk) Needs an ordered category Scatter / bubble X, Y, Size, Details, Play axis Relationships and outliers Add Details so each point is an entity Treemap Group, Values Hierarchical share of total Many tiny rectangles are unreadable Donut / pie Legend, Values 2–3 parts More than 4 slices = use a bar Matrix Rows, Columns, Values Pivot tables with drill and subtotals Use conditional formatting for heat maps Table Columns Exact values, detail list Keep it short; a table is not a dashboard KPI / Gauge Value, Target, Trend axis Progress to a target Gauges waste space; prefer KPI cards Funnel Stage, Values Drop-off between stages Map / Filled map Location, Size, Legend Geography Geocoding needs clean place names ( City, State, Country ) Slicer Field Filter the page Styles: list, dropdown, tiles, between (range), "}, {"i": "powerbi-06", "t": "Power BI Service, Connectivity Modes, Security & Capstone", "k": "Power BI", "f": "lessons/powerbi-06-power-bi-service-connectivity-modes-security-and-capstone.html", "s": " Connectivity modes How does Power BI get its data: copy it into the model (Import) or ask the source each time (DirectQuery)? Import copies data into Power BI's compressed in-memory model; DirectQuery sends a query to the source for every interaction. Mode Data stored in Power BI? Refresh Typical use Import Yes (compressed copy) Scheduled (up to 8/day Pro, 48/day Premium) Most reports; Excel, CSV, SQL DirectQuery No Live per interaction Large SQL databases, near-real-time dashboards Live connection No: connects to an existing semantic model (Analysis Services or a published Power BI dataset) Live One governed model shared by many reports Composite model Mix: some tables Import, some DirectQuery, plus other semantic models Both Large fact in DirectQuery, small dimensions imported (dual storage mode) Direct Lake (Fabric) Reads Delta tables in OneLake directly Automatic on data change Import-like speed with no copy; Fabric only Tip A practical rule Start with Import . Move to DirectQuery or Direct Lake only if data volume or freshness requirements force you to. Reduce the data first: only needed columns, aggregated tables, filtered date range. Publishing and the Service Step by step Publish a report In Desktop: Home → Publish → sign in with a work/school account → choose a workspace (not \"My workspace\" if you want to share). The Service now has a semantic model (the data and model) and a report (the visuals): the same objects in two items. Open the report in the browser, check "}, {"i": "ai-analytics-01", "t": "Generative AI for Analysts: How It Works, Prompting & AI vs GenAI vs Agentic AI", "k": "AI Analytics", "f": "lessons/ai-analytics-01-generative-ai-for-analysts-how-it-works-prompting-and-ai-vs.html", "s": " The AI family tree How the terms relate. Each ring is a subset of the one before it. Agentic AI uses generative models as its brain but adds goals, tools and loops. Term What it means Example AI Machines doing tasks that normally need human intelligence Rule-based chess engine, spam filter Machine learning (ML) Systems that learn patterns from data instead of following hand-written rules Predict loan default from past loans Deep learning ML with many-layered neural networks Image recognition, speech-to-text Generative AI (GenAI) Models that create new content (text, images, code, audio) ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Copilot, Midjourney LLM Large language model: the GenAI engine for text and code GPT, Claude, Gemini, Llama Agentic AI An LLM plus goals, tools and a loop : it plans, calls tools (run SQL, search, edit files), checks results and continues until the goal is met An analyst agent that explores a database and writes the report AGI Hypothetical AI at human level across tasks Not here yet; ignore the hype AI vs GenAI vs agentic AI Traditional ML Generative AI Agentic AI Output A prediction (number, label) New content (text, code, image) A completed multi-step task Input Structured data A prompt (text, files, images) A goal in plain language Control You train and deploy a model You ask once, get one answer The system decides the next step Example \"Will this customer churn?\" → 0.82 \"Summarise these reviews\" → paragraph \"Find why sales fell in Q3 and draft a memo\" → runs queri"}, {"i": "ai-analytics-02", "t": "AI with SQL: Text-to-SQL, Docker, Metabase & MinusX", "k": "AI Analytics", "f": "lessons/ai-analytics-02-ai-with-sql-text-to-sql-docker-metabase-and-minusx.html", "s": " What is text-to-SQL? Text-to-SQL turns a business question in plain language into a SQL query: \"Which region had the highest profit in 2025?\" → SELECT region, SUM(profit) … . LLMs are good at SQL syntax, but correct SQL for your database needs your context. Text-to-SQL pipeline. Quality depends mostly on the context you supply, not on the model alone. What goes wrong without context Problem Example Prevention Wrong tables/columns Uses customer_name in orders , where it does not exist Provide the schema (tables, columns, types, keys) Wrong grain / fan-out Joins orders to items and then COUNT(*) for \"orders\" State the grain: \"one row per order line\"; add \"count distinct order_id\" rule Wrong definitions \"Active customer\", \"net sales\", \"this year\" Provide a metric dictionary Dialect mismatch LIMIT instead of TOP in SQL Server Name the database and version Dates Uses YEAR(date) = 2025 (slow) or the wrong fiscal year State calendar vs fiscal year and preferred patterns Hallucinated values WHERE region = 'Northern' when data says North Provide sample values for categorical columns Unsafe actions DELETE / DROP suggestions Read-only connection; review before running A schema prompt that works Text Copy You are a senior analytics engineer. Write SQL for Microsoft SQL Server 2022. SCHEMA (all in dbo): customers(customer_id PK, customer_name, segment, city, state, region) - segment in (&#x27;Consumer&#x27;,&#x27;Corporate&#x27;,&#x27;Home Office&#x27;); region in (&#x27;North&#x27;,&#x2"}, {"i": "ai-analytics-03", "t": "AI in Excel & Office: Copilot, Gemini, Claude, Python in Excel & Agents", "k": "AI Analytics", "f": "lessons/ai-analytics-03-ai-in-excel-and-office-copilot-gemini-claude-python-in-excel.html", "s": " The AI-in-Excel landscape Four ways AI shows up in spreadsheets. Step 0: make your workbook AI-ready AI assistants can only reason about data they can see and parse . The same rules that make pivot tables work (Excel Lesson 1) make AI work: Convert data to an Excel Table ( Ctrl + T ) with a clear name ( tblSales ) and one header row. Descriptive column names ( order_date , sales_inr ) instead of Col1 ; add units. No merged cells, blank rows or notes inside the data; keep totals outside. One sheet per dataset; a small Data dictionary sheet that defines columns and metrics (AI reads it too). Remove or mask sensitive columns before connecting an AI service. Save to OneDrive/SharePoint (Copilot needs cloud-saved files with AutoSave on). Copilot in Excel Needs a Microsoft 365 Copilot licence and your data formatted as a Table or range with headers. Task Example prompt Explain \"Explain what column H does and how it is calculated\" Formulas \"Add a column that shows profit margin %\" / \"Write a formula that looks up the manager's name from the Managers table\" Insights \"Show me insights about sales by region\" → Copilot adds pivot tables and charts Highlight \"Highlight rows where discount &gt; 20% and profit is negative\" (conditional formatting) Sort / filter \"Sort by sales descending and filter to Technology\" Pivot &amp; charts \"Create a pivot table of sales by category and year with YoY %\" Clean up \"Fix the inconsistent city names\" / \"Split full name into first and last name\" Analyse "}, {"i": "ai-analytics-04", "t": "The AI-Assisted Analyst Workflow, Agents & Governance (Capstone)", "k": "AI Analytics", "f": "lessons/ai-analytics-04-the-ai-assisted-analyst-workflow-agents-and-governance-capst.html", "s": " The AI-assisted analyst workflow Seven steps. AI accelerates several of them, but the analyst owns the first and the last two. Step What you do Where AI helps Your responsibility 1. Frame Decision, audience, metric definitions, constraints Brainstorm questions, critique your framing Own the decision question 2. Brief Schema, definitions, sample rows, rules Turn notes into a clean brief No sensitive data; correct definitions 3. Analyse Profile, clean, aggregate, model Draft SQL/Python, explain code, suggest checks Run it; read it 4. Verify Reconcile totals, test edge cases, second method Write test queries, find anomalies Sign off the numbers 5. Visualise Choose and label charts Suggest chart types, write plotting code Honest, readable charts 6. Write Insight, caveat, action Draft commentary, simplify language Every claim traceable to a number 7. Recommend Action, owner, metric, date Pre-mortem: \"how could this fail?\" Business judgement Case: why do telecom customers leave? Case study Brief A telecom company has 3,000 subscribers in telecom_churn.csv (tenure, contract type, internet service, payment method, charges, support calls, churn Yes/No). The retention head asks: Who is most likely to churn, and what should we do about it? We will follow the workflow and use AI-style assistance where it helps, checking everything. Steps 1–2: frame and brief Text Copy Decision: choose which customers get a retention offer next quarter (budget: 300 offers). Metric: churn rate = churners "}, {"i": "python-01", "t": "Introduction to Python: Why, Install & Jupyter Notebooks", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-01-introduction-to-python-why-install-and-jupyter-notebooks.html", "s": " What is programming? A program is a set of precise instructions a computer follows. Computers only understand machine code (0s and 1s), so we write instructions in a programming language that humans can read, and a translator turns them into something the machine can run. Languages sit on levels. High-level languages like Python are close to human language; low-level ones are close to the machine. Compiled vs interpreted Compiled (C, C++, Rust, Go) Interpreted (Python, JavaScript, R) How it runs Whole program translated to machine code before running Translated and executed line by line while running Speed Usually faster at run time Slower per operation, much faster to develop Errors Many caught before running Some appear only when that line runs Output A separate executable file Source file runs under an interpreter Python is usually called interpreted . In reality CPython (the standard version) first compiles your code to bytecode ( .pyc files) and then a virtual machine executes it. Python code that needs speed calls libraries written in C (NumPy, pandas), so data work is fast anyway. Why Python for data? The Python data ecosystem. You will learn each of these libraries in this track. Property of Python Meaning Readable Indentation and English-like keywords; fewer lines than most languages Interpreted, dynamically typed No compile step; variables take the type of the value they hold High level Memory is managed for you (garbage collection) Multi-paradigm Procedural, objec"}, {"i": "python-02", "t": "Variables, Data Types, Type Conversion, print(), f-strings & input()", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-02-variables-data-types-type-conversion-print-f-strings-and-inp.html", "s": " Variables A variable is a name that refers to a value stored in memory. You create it by assigning with = : no declaration, no type needed. Python Copy price = 1250 # an int tax_rate = 0.18 # a float product = &quot;Laptop&quot; # a str in_stock = True # a bool total = price * ( 1 + tax_rate) print (product, &quot;costs&quot; , total) Output Laptop costs 1475.0 A variable is a label attached to an object. Re-assigning moves the label; it does not change the old object. Naming rules and conventions Rule Example Letters, digits and _ only; cannot start with a digit sales_2025 ✓, 2025_sales ✗ Case-sensitive Total and total are different Cannot be a keyword ( if , for , class , None , True …) class ✗, class_name ✓ Convention : snake_case for variables and functions, PascalCase for classes, UPPER_CASE for constants monthly_income , CustomerOrder , GST_RATE Use meaningful names avg_order_value not aov2 or x Avoid shadowing built-ins Do not name variables list , sum , max , str , id , input Python Copy a = b = c = 0 # chained assignment x, y, z = 1 , 2.5 , &quot;three&quot; # multiple assignment (tuple unpacking) x, y = y, x # swap without a temp variable print (a, b, c, x, y, z) Output 0 0 0 2.5 1 three Data types Everything in Python is an object with a type . Check it with type() . Type Name Example Notes int Integer 42 , -7 , 1_000_000 Unlimited size float Decimal number 3.14 , 2e-3 64-bit binary floating point str Text \"Pune\" , 'Pune' Immutable sequence of characters bool Bool"}, {"i": "python-03", "t": "Operators, Strings, Indexing, Slicing & String Methods", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-03-operators-strings-indexing-slicing-and-string-methods.html", "s": " Operators Arithmetic Operator Meaning Example Result + - * Add, subtract, multiply 7 * 3 21 / True division (always float) 7 / 2 3.5 // Floor division 7 // 2 , -7 // 2 3, −4 % Remainder (modulo) 7 % 2 , -7 % 3 1, 2 ** Power 2 ** 10 1024 Python Copy print ( 7 / 2 , 7 // 2 , - 7 // 2 , 7 % 2 , - 7 % 3 , 2 ** 10 , 9 ** 0.5 ) print ( divmod ( 17 , 5 )) # (quotient, remainder) in one call hours = 135 ; print ( f&quot;{hours // 60} h {hours % 60} min&quot; ) # split minutes into hours and minutes print ( 10 % 3 == 1 , 12 % 2 == 0 ) # test odd/even and divisibility Output 3.5 3 -4 1 2 1024 3.0 (3, 2) 2 h 15 min True True Comparison and logical Python Copy x = 15 print (x &gt; 10 , x == 15 , x != 15 , 10 &lt; x &lt;= 20 ) # chained comparison: 10 &lt; x and x &lt;= 20 print (x &gt; 10 and x &lt; 20 , x &lt; 5 or x &gt; 12 , not x &gt; 10 ) print ( &quot;apple&quot; &lt; &quot;banana&quot; , &quot;Zebra&quot; &lt; &quot;apple&quot; ) # strings compare by character codes: uppercase before lowercase Output True True False True True True False True True Short-circuit evaluation : A and B stops at A if A is falsy; A or B stops at A if A is truthy. They return the deciding operand , not just True/False: Python Copy print ( 0 or &quot;default&quot; , &quot;&quot; or &quot;n/a&quot; , &quot;x&quot; and &quot;y&quot; , None and 1 ) name = &quot;&quot; ; print (name or &quot;Anonymous&quot; ) # a common idiom for default values print ( 5 &gt; 3 and print ( &quot;evaluated&quot; ) is None ) # "}, {"i": "python-04", "t": "Control Flow: if, elif, else, Nested Conditions & match", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-04-control-flow-if-elif-else-nested-conditions-and-match.html", "s": " Decisions and indentation A program normally runs line by line. Control flow lets it choose between paths. In Python, a colon : starts a block and indentation (4 spaces by convention) says which lines belong to it. An if statement tests a condition. If it is True the indented block runs; otherwise Python moves on (or runs else). Python Copy marks = 82 if marks &gt;= 40 : print ( &quot;Pass&quot; ) print ( &quot;Well done!&quot; ) print ( &quot;This line always runs&quot; ) Output Pass Well done! This line always runs Python Copy if True : print ( &quot;not indented&quot; ) # IndentationError: expected an indented block after &#x27;if&#x27; statement Common mistakes Indentation rules Use 4 spaces (never mix tabs and spaces). Everything in the same block has the same indentation . A missing colon or wrong indentation causes SyntaxError / IndentationError . if / elif / else Python Copy marks = 82 if marks &gt;= 90 : grade = &quot;A+&quot; elif marks &gt;= 75 : grade = &quot;A&quot; elif marks &gt;= 60 : grade = &quot;B&quot; elif marks &gt;= 40 : grade = &quot;C&quot; else : grade = &quot;Fail&quot; print (marks, &quot;-&gt;&quot; , grade) Output 82 -&gt; A Python checks the conditions top to bottom and runs the first one that is true, then skips the rest. This is Excel's nested IF / IFS and SQL's CASE WHEN (Excel Lesson 5, SQL Lesson 7). Common mistakes Order matters If you test marks &gt;= 40 first, an 82 gets \"C\" because the first true branch wins. Put the most specific / st"}, {"i": "python-05", "t": "Loops: while, for, range, break, continue & Patterns", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-05-loops-while-for-range-break-continue-and-patterns.html", "s": " Why loops? A loop repeats a block of code. Processing 10,000 rows, trying each file in a folder, retrying a failed request, or checking each number in a list are all loops. Two kinds of loop. for repeats over a known sequence; while repeats as long as a condition stays true. The while loop Python Copy count = 1 while count &lt;= 5 : print ( &quot;Count:&quot; , count) count += 1 # without this line the loop would never end! print ( &quot;Done&quot; ) Output Count: 1 Count: 2 Count: 3 Count: 4 Count: 5 Done Every while loop needs: (1) a starting value, (2) a condition, (3) a change inside the loop that eventually makes the condition False. Python Copy # Compound growth: how many years until 1,00,000 doubles at 8%? amount, years = 100000 , 0 while amount &lt; 200000 : amount *= 1.08 years += 1 print ( f&quot;{years} years -&gt; {amount:,.0f}&quot; ) # the &#x27;rule of 72&#x27;: 72 / 8 = 9 years Output 10 years -&gt; 215,892 while True with break Use an \"infinite\" loop that you leave deliberately when the input is right: Python Copy attempts = [ &quot;abc&quot; , &quot;-4&quot; , &quot;12&quot; ] # simulated user answers i = 0 while True : answer = attempts[i]; i += 1 if answer.isdigit () and int (answer) &gt; 0 : print ( &quot;Accepted:&quot; , answer); break print ( &quot;Invalid:&quot; , answer) Output Invalid: abc Invalid: -4 Accepted: 12 The for loop for item in sequence: takes each item in turn. Python Copy for city in [ &quot;Pune&quot; , &quot;Delhi&quot; , &quot;Kochi"}, {"i": "python-06", "t": "Lists: Creating, Slicing, Methods, Sorting & Comprehensions", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-06-lists-creating-slicing-methods-sorting-and-comprehensions.html", "s": " What is a list? A list is an ordered, mutable collection of items written in square brackets. Items can be of any type, and may repeat. Python Copy cities = [ &quot;Pune&quot; , &quot;Delhi&quot; , &quot;Kochi&quot; , &quot;Delhi&quot; ] mixed = [ 101 , &quot;Kavya&quot; , 97000.5 , True , None ] empty = [] print (cities, len (cities), type (cities).__name__) print ( list ( &quot;data&quot; ), list ( range ( 5 )), list (( 1 , 2 , 3 )), [ 0 ] * 5 ) Output [&#x27;Pune&#x27;, &#x27;Delhi&#x27;, &#x27;Kochi&#x27;, &#x27;Delhi&#x27;] 4 list [&#x27;d&#x27;, &#x27;a&#x27;, &#x27;t&#x27;, &#x27;a&#x27;] [0, 1, 2, 3, 4] [1, 2, 3] [0, 0, 0, 0, 0] A list is a row of numbered boxes. Items are referenced by position; the list itself can grow, shrink and change. Indexing and slicing Works exactly like strings (Lesson 3), but lists can also be changed . Python Copy nums = [ 10 , 20 , 30 , 40 , 50 , 60 ] print (nums[ 0 ], nums[- 1 ], nums[ 1 : 4 ], nums[: 2 ], nums[- 2 :], nums[:: 2 ], nums[::- 1 ]) nums[ 0 ] = 99 # change one item nums[ 1 : 3 ] = [ 21 , 31 , 32 ] # replace a slice (the length may change) print (nums) print ( 3 in nums, 99 in nums, nums.count ( 40 ), nums.index ( 40 )) Output 10 60 [20, 30, 40] [10, 20] [50, 60] [10, 30, 50] [60, 50, 40, 30, 20, 10] [99, 21, 31, 32, 40, 50, 60] False True 1 4 Adding and removing items Method What it does Example Returns append(x) Add one item at the end a.append(5) None extend(iterable) or += Add all items from another sequence a.extend([6,"}, {"i": "python-07", "t": "Tuples & Sets: Immutable Records, Unpacking and Set Algebra", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-07-tuples-and-sets-immutable-records-unpacking-and-set-algebra.html", "s": " Tuples A tuple is an ordered, immutable sequence written with commas (brackets are usually optional). Python Copy point = ( 28.61 , 77.20 ) single = ( 5 ,) # the trailing comma makes a one-item tuple empty = () rec = &quot;E101&quot; , &quot;Kavya Iyer&quot; , &quot;HR&quot; , 97000 # packing: no brackets needed print (point, type (single).__name__, type (( 5 )).__name__, rec[ 1 ], rec[- 1 ], rec[ 1 : 3 ], len (rec)) print ( rec.count ( &quot;HR&quot; ), rec.index ( &quot;HR&quot; ), ( 1 , 2 ) + ( 3 ,), ( 0 ,) * 3 , 5 in ( 1 , 5 , 9 )) try : rec[ 0 ] = &quot;E102&quot; except TypeError as e: print ( &quot;Error:&quot; , e) Output (28.61, 77.2) tuple int Kavya Iyer 97000 (&#x27;Kavya Iyer&#x27;, &#x27;HR&#x27;) 4 1 2 (1, 2, 3) (0, 0, 0) True Error: &#x27;tuple&#x27; object does not support item assignment Unpacking Python Copy emp_id, name, dept, salary = rec # one name per item a, b = 1 , 2 ; a, b = b, a # swap first, *others = ( 10 , 20 , 30 , 40 ) # * collects the rest into a list *head, last = ( 10 , 20 , 30 , 40 ) x, (y, z) = 1 , ( 2 , 3 ) # nested unpacking _, dept_only, *_ = rec # _ means &quot;ignore&quot; print (emp_id, name, a, b, first, others, head, last, x, y, z, dept_only) for city, sales in [( &quot;Pune&quot; , 120 ), ( &quot;Delhi&quot; , 95 )]: # unpack in loops print (city, sales) Output E101 Kavya Iyer 2 1 10 [20, 30, 40] [10, 20, 30] 40 1 2 3 Kavya Iyer Pune 120 Delhi 95 Why tuples? Use Example Fixed records (lat, lon) , (year, month) , a row from a datab"}, {"i": "python-08", "t": "Dictionaries: Key-Value Data, Counting and Grouping", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-08-dictionaries-key-value-data-counting-and-grouping.html", "s": " What is a dictionary? A dictionary ( dict ) maps keys to values : look up by name instead of by position. It keeps insertion order (Python 3.7+), and lookups are O(1) on average. A dictionary is a set of key → value pairs. The key is hashed, so the lookup does not scan the data. Python Copy emp = { &quot;name&quot; : &quot;Kavya Iyer&quot; , &quot;dept&quot; : &quot;HR&quot; , &quot;salary&quot; : 97000 } print (emp[ &quot;name&quot; ], emp.get ( &quot;dept&quot; ), emp.get ( &quot;city&quot; ), emp.get ( &quot;city&quot; , &quot;unknown&quot; ), len (emp), &quot;salary&quot; in emp) try : emp[ &quot;city&quot; ] except KeyError as e: print ( &quot;KeyError:&quot; , e) Output Kavya Iyer HR None unknown 3 True KeyError: &#x27;city&#x27; d[key] raises KeyError when missing; d.get(key, default) never does. Changing a dictionary Python Copy emp[ &quot;city&quot; ] = &quot;Pune&quot; # add emp[ &quot;salary&quot; ] += 3000 # update emp.update ({ &quot;dept&quot; : &quot;Finance&quot; , &quot;grade&quot; : &quot;M2&quot; }) removed = emp.pop ( &quot;grade&quot; ) # remove and return del emp[ &quot;city&quot; ] print (emp, removed) print ( emp.setdefault ( &quot;level&quot; , &quot;L3&quot; ), emp.setdefault ( &quot;dept&quot; , &quot;X&quot; ), emp) # set only if missing Output {&#x27;name&#x27;: &#x27;Kavya Iyer&#x27;, &#x27;dept&#x27;: &#x27;Finance&#x27;, &#x27;salary&#x27;: 100000} M2 L3 Finance {&#x27;name&#x27;: &#x27;Kavya Iyer&#x27;, &#x27;dept&#x27;: &#x27;Finance&#x27;, "}, {"i": "python-09", "t": "Functions: Reusable Code, Arguments, Scope and Docstrings", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-09-functions-reusable-code-arguments-scope-and-docstrings.html", "s": " Why functions? A function is a named, reusable block of code. It removes copy-paste, makes code testable, and lets you give a name to an idea ( clean_phone , margin_pct ). A function takes inputs (arguments), runs its body, and gives back an output (return value). Python Copy def greet (name): return f&quot;Hello, {name}!&quot; print ( greet ( &quot;Asha&quot; ), greet ( &quot;Ravi&quot; )) Output Hello, Asha! Hello, Ravi! Parameters and arguments Python Copy def margin_pct (profit, sales, decimals= 1 ): &quot;&quot;&quot;Profit as a percentage of sales.&quot;&quot;&quot; return round ( 100 * profit / sales, decimals) print ( margin_pct ( 25 , 200 )) # positional print ( margin_pct (sales= 200 , profit= 25 )) # keyword: order does not matter print ( margin_pct ( 25 , 200 , decimals= 3 )) # default overridden Output 12.5 12.5 12.5 Kind Example Notes Positional f(1, 2) Matched by order Keyword f(b=2, a=1) Matched by name; clearer Default def f(a, b=10) Optional argument; defaults go last *args def f(*args) Extra positional arguments as a tuple **kwargs def f(**kwargs) Extra keyword arguments as a dict Keyword-only def f(a, *, b) b must be named Python Copy def total (*nums): return sum (nums) def describe (**info): return &quot;, &quot; . join ( f&quot;{k}={v}&quot; for k, v in info.items ()) def report (title, *values, sep= &quot; | &quot; , **opts): return title + &quot;: &quot; + sep.join ( map (str, values)) + f&quot; {opts}&quot; print ( total ( 1 , 2 , 3 ), total (), des"}, {"i": "python-10", "t": "Comprehensions, Lambda, map/filter and Generators", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-10-comprehensions-lambda-map-filter-and-generators.html", "s": " List comprehensions A comprehension builds a collection from another in one expression: [expression for item in iterable if condition] . A comprehension reads like the sentence \"give me X for each item, if the condition holds\". Python Copy nums = [ 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 ] squares = [n * n for n in nums] evens = [n for n in nums if n % 2 == 0 ] labels = [ &quot;even&quot; if n % 2 == 0 else &quot;odd&quot; for n in nums] # if/else goes BEFORE the for loop_version = [] for n in nums: if n % 2 == 0 : loop_version.append (n * n) print (squares, evens, labels, [n * n for n in nums if n % 2 == 0 ] == loop_version) Output [1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36] [2, 4, 6] [&#x27;odd&#x27;, &#x27;even&#x27;, &#x27;odd&#x27;, &#x27;even&#x27;, &#x27;odd&#x27;, &#x27;even&#x27;] True Python Copy words = [ &quot; Pune &quot; , &quot;delhi&quot; , &quot; KOCHI&quot; , &quot;goa &quot; ] print ([ w.strip (). title () for w in words]) print ([(x, y) for x in ( 1 , 2 ) for y in ( &quot;a&quot; , &quot;b&quot; )]) # two loops matrix = [[ 1 , 2 , 3 ], [ 4 , 5 , 6 ]] print ([v for row in matrix for v in row], [ list (col) for col in zip (*matrix)]) # flatten, transpose Output [&#x27;Pune&#x27;, &#x27;Delhi&#x27;, &#x27;Kochi&#x27;, &#x27;Goa&#x27;] [(1, &#x27;a&#x27;), (1, &#x27;b&#x27;), (2, &#x27;a&#x27;), (2, &#x27;b&#x27;)] [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6] [[1, 4], [2, 5], [3, 6]] Set and dictionary comprehensions Python Copy print ({ len (w) for w in [ &quot;tea&quot; , &quot;cup&quot; , &quot;mug&quot; , &quot;plate&qu"}];